        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-disclaimer-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The advice offered below is based on the current "
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "State of the 2014 DnD 5e Character Optimization Meta",
              "href": "https://rpgbot.net/dnd5/characters/state-of-the-meta/"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " as of when the article was last updated. Keep in mind that the state of the meta periodically changes as new source materials are released, and the article will be updated accordingly as time allows."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-disclaimer-4",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "We will not include 3rd-party content, Unearthed Arcnaa content, or homebrew in guides for official content because we can’t assume that your game will allow them."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-versions",
      "heading": "Goliath Versions",
      "sourceAnchor": "versions",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-versions-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The original version of the Goliath was published in the Elemental Evil\n\tPlayer’s Companion. It was later updated in Icewind Dale: Rime of the\n\tFrostmaiden updated this original version to add resistance to cold damage."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-versions-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The introduction of the custom origin rules gave us the third version of the\n\tgoliath, freeing the Goliath’s skill proficiency and ability score increases\n\tso that the Goliath is no longer locked into Strength-based melee builds/"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-versions-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Most recently, Mordenkainen Presents Monsters of the Multiverse updated the\n\tGoliath to match the updated race design concepts. Goliaths get the standard\n\t+2/+1 or three +1 increases, and Stone’s Endurance was changed from once per\n\tShort or Long Rest to PB/Long Rest. Unfortunately, the Goliath’s skill\n\tproficiency is once again restricted to Athletics, which provides very little\n\tbenefit to anything not Strength-based. Still, it’s one skill proficiency, and\n\tthe core races have been doing just fine without racial skill proficiencies\n\tfor years, so if you have one useless skill it’s not going to kill you."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Goliath Classes (Customizable Origins and MMoM)",
      "sourceAnchor": "classes-custom-origin",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classes-custom-origin-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "This section assumes that you’re using the option updated versions of the race, including the “"
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "Customizing Your Origin",
              "href": "https://www.dndbeyond.com/sources/tcoe/character-options#CustomizingYourOrigin"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "” rules presented in Tasha’s Cauldron of Everything and/or the updated version published in "
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "Monsters of the Multiverse",
              "href": "https://www.dndbeyond.com/sources/motm/fantastical-races"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ". Because the race changed so little between those two versions, I have decided to combine these two sections. If you’re not using those rules, scroll down to the “Classic Rules” section."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-artificer",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Artificer",
      "sourceAnchor": "artificer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-artificer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Artificers are famously durable due to their ability to stack AC infusions on\n\ttop of heavy armor, but they still only have d8 hit points, and Stone’s\n\tEndurance can do quite a bit to pad that small pool of hp. Cold resistance is\n\tsomewhat redundant since artificers can cast Absorb Elements, but an\n\tadditional resistance means one less option for enemies to slip past your\n\tdefenses."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-barbarian",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Barbarian",
      "sourceAnchor": "barbarian",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-barbarian-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Goliath already made a perfect barbarian, and neither the custom origin\n\trules nor Monsters of the Multiverse did anything to hurt that prospect.\n\tDamage resistance, a free skill proficiency in one of the Barbarian’s best\n\tskills, and Stone’s Endurance all work wonderfully for the Barbarian."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-barbarian-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Remember that damage resistance is applied after any other modifiers to the\n\tdamage recieved, so Stone’s Endurance applies before damage is halved for\n\tdamage resistance. This means that it’s less effective while raging, but that\n\tdoesn’t make it bad by any means."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-bard",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Bard",
      "sourceAnchor": "bard",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-bard-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Bards almost never belong in melee, and Stone’s Endurance doesn’t change\n\tthat. While bards do get light armor (medium for College of Valor) and more\n\thit points than sorcerers or wizards, that doesn’t suddenly make weapons a\n\tgood idea in the face of the Bard’s challenges when trying to use weapons."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-cleric",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Cleric",
      "sourceAnchor": "cleric",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-cleric-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "With up to heavy armor and the best healing options in the game, the Cleric\n\thas no shortage of hit points. Stone’s Endurance is great for front-line\n\tbuilds, especially if you’re in a small party and you’re the only Defender,\n\tbut otherwise it’s not going to be hugely impactful."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-druid",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Druid",
      "sourceAnchor": "druid",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-druid-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Druid’s frailty is among the class’s biggest problems, and Stone’s\n\tEndurance does a lot to make up that shortcoming. It’s not clear if it works\n\twhile Wild Shaped, unfortunately."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-fighter",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Fighter",
      "sourceAnchor": "fighter",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-fighter-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Fighters get heavy armor and plenty of hit points, but Stone’s Endurance adds\n\tan extra layer of durability on top of that which you can use to make\n\ttwo-handed weapons safer to use. Athletics is a great skill for two-handed\n\tweapon builds so that you can take advantage of the grapple/shove combo."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-monk",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Monk",
      "sourceAnchor": "monk",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-monk-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Monk is locked into melee, and at low levels before you get Ability Score Increases to raise your Dexterity (and therefore your AC), the Monk’s combination of d8 hit dice and poor AC is a significant liability. Stone’s Endurance provides a significant boost to your durability. If you’re using the custom origin rules, go +2 Dex/+1 Wis and reassign the skill proficiency into something like Perception. If you’re using the Goliath in Monsters of the Multiverse, go for 16 in Dex/Con/Wis and pretend that Athletics isn’t a thing unless you go for Way of the Astral Self and want to grapple."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-paladin",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Paladin",
      "sourceAnchor": "paladin",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-paladin-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Goliath’s traits combined with the Paladin’s already excellent durability\n\tmake for an extremely durable character. However, since you’re so durable\n\tyou’re getting into tank falacy territory, so consider an aggressive subclass\n\tlike Oath of Conquest or at least plan to use two-handed weapons."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-ranger",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Ranger",
      "sourceAnchor": "ranger",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-ranger-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Rangers get worse armor than the Fighter or the Paladin, fewer hit points\n\tthan the Barbarian (not to mention damage resistance), they can’t Dodge as a\n\tBonus Action like the Monk, and don’t get Uncanny Dodge to mitigate damage\n\tlike the Rogue does. Stone’s Endurance and cold resistance do a lot to help\n\tmake up that gap in capabilities, and an extra skill reduces the skill gap\n\tbetween the Ranger and the Rogue. If you’re using custom origin rules your\n\tskill options are wide open, but if you’re using the updated version in\n\tMonsters of the Multiverse you’ll likely only find Athletics useful if you\n\tbuld around Strength."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-rogue",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Rogue",
      "sourceAnchor": "rogue",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-rogue-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Rogues get Uncanny Dodge, and as great as Stone’s Endurance is, Uncanny Dodge\n\tis considerably better. If you want a skill and cold resistance, consider the\n\tSea Elf."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-sorcerer",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Sorcerer",
      "sourceAnchor": "sorcerer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-sorcerer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Stone’s Endurance is great on a class with no armor proficiency and tiny hit\n\tdice, but if you do a good job avoiding drawing attacks, Stone’s Endurance\n\twill rarely matter. You could use that to justify not investing in\n\tConstitution, but where else are you going to put those resources? Sorcerers\n\tare among the most SAD classes in the game."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-warlock",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Warlock",
      "sourceAnchor": "warlock",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-warlock-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Hexblades are very effective offensively, but in medium armor and with d8 hit\n\tpoints they’re frail compared to many other front-line builds. Stone’s\n\tEndurance can make a huge difference there, allowing you to remain on the\n\tfront lines much longer than most warlocks can. For other warlocks, you\n\tshouldn’t be drawing enough fire to need Stone’s Endurance."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-wizard",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Wizard",
      "sourceAnchor": "wizard",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-wizard-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Stone’s Endurance is great on a class with no armor proficiency and tiny hit\n\tdice, but if you do a good job avoiding drawing attacks, Stone’s Endurance\n\twill rarely matter. You could use that to justify not investing in\n\tConstitution, but where else are you going to put those resources? Wizards are\n\tamong the most SAD classes in the game."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Goliath Classes (Default Rules)",
      "sourceAnchor": "classes-default",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classes-default-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "This section assumes that you’re "
            },
            {
              "type": "emphasis",
              "text": "not"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " using the option “Customizing Your Origin” rules presented in Tasha’s Cauldron of Everything or the updated version of the race published in Mordenkainen’s Monsters of the Multiverse. If you are using those rules, scroll up to the previous section."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-artificer-2",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Artificer",
      "sourceAnchor": "artificer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-artificer-2-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "No Intelligence increase."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-barbarian-2",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Barbarian",
      "sourceAnchor": "barbarian",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-barbarian-2-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Strength and Constitution are perfect, and if there’s any class that needs\n\tStone’s Endurance it’s the Barbarian. Negating 1d12+Con damage is huge since\n\tthe Barbarian often has lower AC than most front-line melee classes, and\n\tReckless Attack compounds that issue."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-bard-2",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Bard",
      "sourceAnchor": "bard",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-bard-2-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Strength-based bards are already hard enough, and the Goliath doesn’t get a\n\tCharisma increase."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-cleric-2",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Cleric",
      "sourceAnchor": "cleric",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-cleric-2-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "A front-line Strength-based cleric can work, especially at low levels before\n\tExtra Attack makes other characters outpace you, but lacking a Wisdom increase\n\twill hurt for a long time."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-druid-2",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Druid",
      "sourceAnchor": "druid",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-druid-2-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Nearly nothing about the Goliath benefits the Druid."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-fighter-2",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Fighter",
      "sourceAnchor": "fighter",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-fighter-2-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Strength, Constitution, Athletics, and Stone’s Endurance. Stone’s Endurance\n\tis a great defensive option that can make it easier to choose two-handed\n\tweapons over using a shield, but honestly the Goliath works on any\n\tStrength-based fighter build."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-monk-2",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Monk",
      "sourceAnchor": "monk",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-monk-2-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "No increases to either Dexterity or Wisdom, and the Monk is too reliant on\n\thaving high scores in those abilities."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-paladin-2",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Paladin",
      "sourceAnchor": "paladin",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-paladin-2-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Goliath works for the Paladin for the same reason that they work for the\n\tFighter. Lacking a Charisma increase isn’t a deal breaker, and proficiency in\n\tAthletics means that you don’t need to choose between Athletics and a Face\n\tskill like Persuasion. Stone’s Endurance is less appealing for the Paladin\n\tthan for the Barbarian or the Fighter since the Paladin has more built-in\n\thealing options, but it’s still fantastic and it’ll keep you alive at low\n\tlevels before those healing options come online."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-ranger-2",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Ranger",
      "sourceAnchor": "ranger",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-ranger-2-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Strength-based rangers are hard, but one of their biggest challenges is\n\trelatively poor AC and needing high scores in several ability scores to fill\n\tout medium armor without cutting into other capabilities. Stone’s Endurance\n\tcan help offset the Ranger’s relative frailty compared to the Fighter, and\n\tAthletics adds and extra skill on top the Ranger’s already higher-than-normal\n\tnumber of skill proficiencies. It’s still not a spectacular build, but it\n\tcould work."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-rogue-2",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Rogue",
      "sourceAnchor": "rogue",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-rogue-2-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Bad ability spread, and none of the Goliath’s traits suppor the Rogue."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-sorcerer-2",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Sorcerer",
      "sourceAnchor": "sorcerer",
      "blocks": [
